            <ArticleTitle>Spinal Cord Injuries and Nerve Dependence in Prostate Cancer.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>812-815</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="pii" ValidYN="Y">S2405-8033(17)30195-4</ELocationID>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.trecan.2017.10.001</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText>Nerves are emerging as drivers of tumorigenesis, as demonstrated in the mouse where denervation suppresses prostate cancer; however, clinical evidence is needed. Patients with spinal cord injuries (SCIs) resulting in functional denervation of the prostate have a lower incidence of prostate cancer. This may constitute a clinical evidence for nerve dependence in human prostate tumorigenesis.</AbstractText>
